#: lessons/answers-key.md:434
msgctxt "s-2aacdef0d7f0-0126"
msgid "F2. Trojan Source: hide a character, then find it"
msgstr ""

#: lessons/answers-key.md:436
msgctxt "s-abff9db7086c-0127"
msgid ""
"⟦CODE_0001⟧ prints ⟦CODE_0002⟧. The first line holds ⟦CODE_0003⟧ followed by U+200B, a character with no shape, so the string has five visible letters (⟦CODE_0004⟧) and one invisible character: it is not equal to ⟦CODE_0005⟧. The second line compares it with the plain word. A reader who sees ⟦CODE_0006⟧ expects ⟦CODE_0007⟧.\n"
"2. The scan prints line 1, ⟦CODE_0008⟧, and there is one more character between the ⟦CODE_0009⟧ and the closing quote that your terminal draws as nothing (that is the point). ⟦CODE_0010⟧ shows ⟦CODE_0011⟧: UTF-8 for U+200B is the three bytes E2 80 8B, which is ⟦CODE_0012⟧ in octal.\n"
"3. ⟦CODE_0013⟧ has no hit, and prints ⟦CODE_0014⟧. The scan fires on the character and on nothing else.\n"
"4. No file in ⟦CODE_0015⟧ matches. APL glyphs are visible, so a reviewer sees them. Banning every non-ASCII character would also ban APL and every non-English name, and it would not stop an attacker who uses an ASCII look-alike. The defense is to ban what has no visible shape. The label is **implementation-permitted**: the rule is written down (⟦CODE_0016⟧, the line that sets ⟦CODE_0017⟧), its mechanism is ⟦CODE_0018⟧ and its Unicode tables, and the character class lists only the characters we know about, so a new invisible character in a future Unicode version would not be caught until the list is updated."
msgstr ""

